// sin/cos rotate the wound rotor's terminal quantities into the stator frame.
from <math> import sin, cos;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  InductionMachine — three-phase squirrel-cage induction (asynchronous)
//  machine, dynamic model in the stator (αβ) frame, with a shaft port
//
//      va ──┐                              ┌─────────────────────┐
//      vb ──┤ star (isolated neutral)      │ C<J>   B·ω    T_em  │── shaft
//      vc ──┘ or delta (delta = 1)         └─────────────────────┘
//
//  The fifth-order model of every textbook (Krause; Simulink's
//  "Asynchronous Machine"), written in the stationary frame so that no
//  rotating transform is needed: the stator quantities ARE the Clarke
//  quantities of the terminals. Rotor quantities are referred to the stator
//  (turns ratio 1). With space vectors x = xα + j·xβ:
//
//      vs = Rs·is + dψs/dt
//      0  = Rr·ir + dψr/dt − j·ωe·ψr                (cage: rotor shorted)
//      ψs = Lls·is + ψm,   ψr = Llr·ir + ψm,   ψm = Lm·im,   im = is + ir
//      ωe = p·ωm
//      T_em = 1.5·p·(ψsα·isβ − ψsβ·isα) = 1.5·p·(ψmα·isβ − ψmβ·isα)
//           = 1.5·p·Lm·(isβ·irα − isα·irβ)          (linear)
//
//  Circuit. Each axis is the machine's T equivalent circuit built from real
//  inductors, so that the flux derivatives are the inductors' own (exactly as
//  PMSM's dq axes are), and the currents are their branch currents:
//
//      s1 ──Lls──┬──Llr── r1        V(s1) = vs − Rs·is   (voltage source)
//                Lm                 V(r1) = −Rr·ir − (speed voltage)
//               gnd
//
//  (The magnetising inductor has a series voltage source that is zero unless
//  Im_sat > 0 — see "Main-flux saturation" below.)
//  The resistive drops are written into the two voltage sources rather than
//  as R elements, so Rs = 0 and Rr = 0 are both allowed (as in PMSM).
//  The rotor speed voltages, from −j·ωe·ψr:
//
//      V(r1α) = −Rr·irα − ωe·ψrβ
//      V(r1β) = −Rr·irβ + ωe·ψrα
//
//  Transforms are amplitude-invariant (Clarke 2/3 scaling), exactly as in
//  PMSM: balanced phase currents of peak Î give |isα + j·isβ| = Î, power is
//  1.5·(vα·iα + vβ·iβ), hence the 1.5 in the torque. A positive-sequence
//  supply (a leads b leads c) turns the field, and the rotor, in the positive
//  direction.
//
//  Steady state. On a balanced sinusoidal supply of electrical frequency
//  ωs = 2π·f the model reduces to the per-phase equivalent circuit
//
//      Vs = (Rs + j·ωs·Lls)·Is + j·ωs·Lm·(Is + Ir)
//      0  = (Rr/s + j·ωs·Llr)·Ir + j·ωs·Lm·(Is + Ir),   s = (ωs − p·ωm)/ωs
//      T_em = 1.5·p·|Ir|²·Rr / (s·ωs)                   (peak phasors)
//
//  and the model reproduces it: torque, current and input power at slips
//  from −0.05 (generating) through breakdown to 1 (locked rotor) agree with
//  this circuit to 1e-6 (trapezoidal, 5 µs ceiling; 2e-4 at 20 µs) — except
//  next to synchronism, where torque ∝ slip magnifies the integrator's own
//  ~(ω·dt)²/12 frequency error: 2e-4 of the torque at s = 0.001 — and a
//  direct-on-line start agrees with an independent integration of the
//  equations above to 6 mrad/s (examples/InductionMachine/dol_start.hvr).
//
//  Terminals. Star (delta = 0, the default): the windings share an isolated
//  neutral, the phase voltages are the terminal voltages less their mean, and
//  no zero-sequence current flows — as in PMSM. Delta (delta = 1): winding
//  "a" lies between terminals a and b, "b" between b and c, "c" between c
//  and a; the winding voltages are the line-to-line voltages and the terminal
//  currents are differences of winding currents. No circulating
//  zero-sequence current is modelled in delta either. The parameters are per
//  WINDING in both cases, so a "400 V Δ / 690 V Y" machine has the same
//  parameters in both connections and is fed 400 V line-to-line in delta.
//
//  Each terminal is a current source to ground, so, as for PMSM:
//    - no phase may be left completely open (a node with only a current
//      source on it is singular); a phase that is to carry no current still
//      needs a path, e.g. a large resistor;
//    - the machine gives the terminals no reference of its own: whatever
//      drives them must tie them to ground somewhere.
//
//  Shaft. Exactly PMSM's convention: V(shaft) = ωm in rad/s, current =
//  torque in N·m; J is a capacitor and B a self-sensing VCCS on the shaft
//  node (so B = 0 is allowed), the electromagnetic torque a current source
//  into it:
//
//      J·dωm/dt = T_em − B·ωm − T_shaft
//
//  T_shaft is the current leaving through the shaft pin into the load.
//  Motoring torque is positive. Loads go from shaft to ground (see pmsm.hvr,
//  and <mechanical> for friction, fan, gear and flexible-shaft parts).
//  Driven above synchronous speed (p·ωm > ωs, s < 0) the machine generates:
//  T_em < 0 and the electrical power reverses.
//
//  Operating point. Under .op the bias point is DC: the inductors are shorts,
//  so a sinusoidal supply is taken at its t = 0 value and the stator carries
//  the DC current V/Rs that implies. That is the true DC solution and not a
//  sinusoidal steady state; a direct-on-line start is a run WITHOUT .op
//  (the machine at rest, all currents zero).
//
//  Solver. A Newton solver (bdf2, ndf2, trapezoidal), as for PMSM.
//
//  Main-flux saturation (optional, Im_sat > 0). The magnetising inductance
//  falls smoothly with the magnitude of the magnetising-current vector:
//
//      ψm = Lm_e·im,   Lm_e = Lm / √(1 + |im|²/Im_sat²)
//
//  so |ψm| = Lm·|im| for |im| ≪ Im_sat, 71 % of that at |im| = Im_sat, and
//  tends to the ceiling Lm·Im_sat. It is isotropic (it depends on |im|, not
//  on the direction), which gives the cross-saturation between α and β that
//  main-flux saturation physically has, and it depends on |im|² only, so it
//  is smooth through im = 0. Implemented as a voltage source
//  d((Lm_e − Lm)·im)/dt in series with the unsaturated Lm inductor, so the
//  default Im_sat = 0 is exactly the linear model. In steady state on a
//  sinusoidal supply |im| is constant and the equivalent circuit holds with
//  Lm_e in place of Lm. Im_sat is a fit parameter, not a datum: fit it to the
//  no-load curve. The ceiling Lm·Im_sat must lie above the rated air-gap flux
//  (≈ V̂s/ωs, peak phase voltage over electrical frequency) or rated voltage
//  cannot be reached at any current: with the ceiling 1.5× the rated flux the
//  rated magnetising current is 1.34× its linear value, with 2× it is 1.15×.
//  The leakage inductances stay linear, and the hard ceiling makes it a poor
//  model far past the knee (a real curve keeps a small slope there).
//
//  Parameters (per winding, referred to the stator)
//      Rs      stator resistance, Ohm (0 allowed)
//      Lls     stator leakage inductance, H (> 0)
//      Rr      rotor resistance, referred, Ohm (0 allowed)
//      Llr     rotor leakage inductance, referred, H (> 0)
//      Lm      magnetising inductance, H (> 0)
//      p       number of pole PAIRS (a 4-pole machine has p = 2)
//      J       rotor inertia, kg·m² (0 allowed — see pmsm.hvr)
//      B       rotor viscous friction, N·m·s/rad (0 allowed)
//      delta   0 = star (default), 1 = delta
//      Im_sat  main-flux saturation current, A peak (0 = linear, the default)
//  From a data sheet's per-phase equivalent circuit at frequency f:
//  Lls = Xls/(2π·f), and likewise Llr, Lm; a delta machine's equivalent
//  circuit is usually already per winding, a star one per phase.
//
//  Outputs (logic)
//      omega_m_out      mechanical speed ωm, rad/s (= V(shaft))
//      i_alpha_out      stator (winding) current, α axis, A (= i of winding a)
//      i_beta_out       stator current, β axis, A
//      psi_r_alpha_out  rotor flux linkage ψrα, Wb (for flux observers / FOC)
//      psi_r_beta_out   rotor flux linkage ψrβ, Wb
//      torque_out       electromagnetic torque T_em, N·m (before friction)
//
//  Not modelled: saturation of the leakage flux (Lls, Llr are constants;
//  main-flux saturation is the optional Im_sat above), deep-bar / double-cage rotor effects (Rr and Llr do not change
//  with slip frequency, so a fit at rated slip overestimates the locked-rotor
//  current and underestimates its torque), iron loss, stray load loss, space
//  harmonics and slotting, temperature, and any zero-sequence path.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

analog module InductionMachine<double Rs, double Lls, double Rr, double Llr,
                               double Lm, double p, double J, double B,
                               double delta = 0, double Im_sat = 0>
    (output double omega_m_out, output double i_alpha_out,
     output double i_beta_out, output double psi_r_alpha_out,
     output double psi_r_beta_out, output double torque_out)
    [wire va, wire vb, wire vc, wire shaft]
{
    wire sa, ma, qa, ra, sb, mb, qb, rb;

    // ── Mechanics, on the shaft node (as PMSM) ──────────────────────────────
    C<J>()                   [shaft, gnd];
    VCCS<B>()                [shaft, gnd, shaft, gnd];

    double omega_m = V(shaft);
    double omega_e = p * omega_m;

    // ── Terminal voltages → winding voltages → αβ (amplitude-invariant) ────
    // Star: winding k sees V(k) less the isolated star point (the mean).
    // Delta: winding a is a–b, winding b is b–c (and c is c–a).
    double v_n = (V(va) + V(vb) + V(vc)) / 3.0;
    double u_a = V(va) - v_n;
    double u_b = V(vb) - v_n;
    if (delta > 0.5) {
        u_a = V(va) - V(vb);
        u_b = V(vb) - V(vc);
    }
    double v_alpha = u_a;
    double v_beta  = (u_a + 2.0 * u_b) / (3.0**0.5);

    // ── T equivalent circuit per axis ───────────────────────────────────────
    double is_a = I(Lls_a);         // stator current, α (s1 → m)
    double is_b = I(Lls_b);
    double ir_a = I(Llr_a);         // rotor current, α (r1 → m)
    double ir_b = I(Llr_b);
    double im_a = I(Lm_a);          // magnetising current = is + ir
    double im_b = I(Lm_b);

    // Main flux ψm = Lm_e·im (a vector along im). Linear: Lm_e = Lm. With
    // Im_sat > 0 it saturates smoothly with the magnetising-current
    // magnitude; the inductor carries the unsaturated Lm and a series source
    // the difference d((Lm_e − Lm)·im)/dt, which is zero in the linear case.
    double lm_e = Lm;
    if (Im_sat > 0.0) {
        lm_e = Lm / ((1.0 + (im_a * im_a + im_b * im_b) / (Im_sat * Im_sat))**0.5);
    }
    double psi_m_a = lm_e * im_a;
    double psi_m_b = lm_e * im_b;
    double v_sat_a = ddt((lm_e - Lm) * im_a);
    double v_sat_b = ddt((lm_e - Lm) * im_b);

    double psi_r_a = Llr * ir_a + psi_m_a;
    double psi_r_b = Llr * ir_b + psi_m_b;

    double v_s_a = v_alpha - Rs * is_a;
    double v_s_b = v_beta  - Rs * is_b;
    double v_r_a = 0.0 - Rr * ir_a - omega_e * psi_r_b;
    double v_r_b = 0.0 - Rr * ir_b + omega_e * psi_r_a;

    voltage_source<>(v_s_a) [sa, gnd];
    L Lls_a<Lls>()          [sa, ma];
    L Lm_a<Lm>()            [ma, qa];
    voltage_source<>(v_sat_a) [qa, gnd];
    L Llr_a<Llr>()          [ra, ma];
    voltage_source<>(v_r_a) [ra, gnd];

    voltage_source<>(v_s_b) [sb, gnd];
    L Lls_b<Lls>()          [sb, mb];
    L Lm_b<Lm>()            [mb, qb];
    voltage_source<>(v_sat_b) [qb, gnd];
    L Llr_b<Llr>()          [rb, mb];
    voltage_source<>(v_r_b) [rb, gnd];

    // ── αβ currents → winding currents → terminal currents ─────────────────
    double i_wa = is_a;
    double i_wb = -0.5 * is_a + ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * is_b;
    double i_wc = -0.5 * is_a - ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * is_b;

    double i_a = i_wa;
    double i_b = i_wb;
    double i_c = i_wc;
    if (delta > 0.5) {
        i_a = i_wa - i_wc;
        i_b = i_wb - i_wa;
        i_c = i_wc - i_wb;
    }

    // Motor convention: positive current flows into the terminal.
    current_source<>(i_a) [va, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_b) [vb, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_c) [vc, gnd];

    // ── Electromagnetic torque, into the shaft ──────────────────────────────
    // T = 1.5·p·(ψsα·isβ − ψsβ·isα) = 1.5·p·(ψmα·isβ − ψmβ·isα)
    double torque = 1.5 * p * (psi_m_a * is_b - psi_m_b * is_a);
    current_source<>(torque) [gnd, shaft];

    omega_m_out     = omega_m;
    i_alpha_out     = is_a;
    i_beta_out      = is_b;
    psi_r_alpha_out = psi_r_a;
    psi_r_beta_out  = psi_r_b;
    torque_out      = torque;
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  InductionMachineWR — wound-rotor (slip-ring) induction machine
//
//  InductionMachine with the rotor winding brought out to three slip-ring
//  terminals ra, rb, rc (star, isolated neutral) instead of shorted:
//
//      vr = Rr·ir + dψr/dt − j·ωe·ψr        (stator frame, referred)
//
//  The rotor terminal quantities live in the ROTOR frame (they turn with the
//  rotor and have slip frequency s·f in steady state). They are Clarke-
//  transformed there and rotated into the stator frame by the rotor's
//  electrical angle
//
//      θr = θ0 + ∫ωe dt,    vr(stator) = vr(rotor)·e^(+jθr),
//                           ir(rotor)  = ir(stator)·e^(−jθr)
//
//  (an integrator state, never wrapped, as PMSM's θe). θ0 = 0 puts rotor
//  phase a on stator phase a at t = 0.
//
//  Everything is referred to the stator with turns ratio 1: the rotor
//  terminals carry the referred voltages and currents. For a machine with an
//  effective stator:rotor turns ratio a, a physical rotor resistor R_phys
//  is R_phys·a² here, and the physical slip-ring voltage is the terminal
//  voltage over a. External rotor resistance per phase simply adds to Rr in
//  the torque–slip curve, which is what starting resistors are for.
//
//  Rotor terminals are current sources to ground like the stator ones, with
//  the same two rules: no rotor phase completely open, and the rotor circuit
//  needs a path to ground — star-connect the rotor resistors to gnd (no
//  current flows in that ground return: the three rotor currents sum to zero).
//  A shorted rotor is not three wires tied together (a node with only
//  current sources on it is singular): use InductionMachine for that.
//
//  Ports [va, vb, vc, ra, rb, rc, shaft]; the stator may be star or delta
//  (delta = 1), the rotor is star. Parameters as InductionMachine (including
//  the optional Im_sat), plus theta0 (initial electrical rotor angle, rad,
//  default 0); pass the defaulted ones by name, e.g. <..., B, delta = 1>. Outputs as
//  InductionMachine plus theta_e_out, the rotor's electrical angle θr (rad,
//  unwrapped). Not modelled: as InductionMachine, plus brush drop and
//  slip-ring resistance.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

analog module InductionMachineWR<double Rs, double Lls, double Rr, double Llr,
                                 double Lm, double p, double J, double B,
                                 double theta0 = 0, double delta = 0,
                                 double Im_sat = 0>
    (output double omega_m_out, output double i_alpha_out,
     output double i_beta_out, output double psi_r_alpha_out,
     output double psi_r_beta_out, output double theta_e_out,
     output double torque_out)
    [wire va, wire vb, wire vc, wire ra, wire rb, wire rc, wire shaft]
{
    wire sa, ma, qa, xa, sb, mb, qb, xb;

    // ── Mechanics, on the shaft node (as PMSM) ──────────────────────────────
    C<J>()                   [shaft, gnd];
    VCCS<B>()                [shaft, gnd, shaft, gnd];

    double omega_m = V(shaft);
    double omega_e = p * omega_m;

    // ── Rotor electrical angle: θr = θ0 + ∫ p·ωm dt ─────────────────────────
    double theta_r = theta0 + idt(omega_e);
    double c_th = cos(theta_r);
    double s_th = sin(theta_r);

    // ── Stator: terminal voltages → αβ (star or delta) ──────────────────────
    double v_n = (V(va) + V(vb) + V(vc)) / 3.0;
    double u_a = V(va) - v_n;
    double u_b = V(vb) - v_n;
    if (delta > 0.5) {
        u_a = V(va) - V(vb);
        u_b = V(vb) - V(vc);
    }
    double v_alpha = u_a;
    double v_beta  = (u_a + 2.0 * u_b) / (3.0**0.5);

    // ── Rotor: slip-ring voltages → rotor-frame xy → stator frame ───────────
    double vr_n = (V(ra) + V(rb) + V(rc)) / 3.0;
    double ur_a = V(ra) - vr_n;
    double ur_b = V(rb) - vr_n;
    double vr_x = ur_a;
    double vr_y = (ur_a + 2.0 * ur_b) / (3.0**0.5);
    double vr_alpha = vr_x * c_th - vr_y * s_th;
    double vr_beta  = vr_x * s_th + vr_y * c_th;

    // ── T equivalent circuit per axis (as InductionMachine) ─────────────────
    double is_a = I(Lls_a);
    double is_b = I(Lls_b);
    double ir_a = I(Llr_a);
    double ir_b = I(Llr_b);
    double im_a = I(Lm_a);
    double im_b = I(Lm_b);

    // Main flux ψm = Lm_e·im (a vector along im). Linear: Lm_e = Lm. With
    // Im_sat > 0 it saturates smoothly with the magnetising-current
    // magnitude; the inductor carries the unsaturated Lm and a series source
    // the difference d((Lm_e − Lm)·im)/dt, which is zero in the linear case.
    double lm_e = Lm;
    if (Im_sat > 0.0) {
        lm_e = Lm / ((1.0 + (im_a * im_a + im_b * im_b) / (Im_sat * Im_sat))**0.5);
    }
    double psi_m_a = lm_e * im_a;
    double psi_m_b = lm_e * im_b;
    double v_sat_a = ddt((lm_e - Lm) * im_a);
    double v_sat_b = ddt((lm_e - Lm) * im_b);

    double psi_r_a = Llr * ir_a + psi_m_a;
    double psi_r_b = Llr * ir_b + psi_m_b;

    double v_s_a = v_alpha - Rs * is_a;
    double v_s_b = v_beta  - Rs * is_b;
    double v_r_a = vr_alpha - Rr * ir_a - omega_e * psi_r_b;
    double v_r_b = vr_beta  - Rr * ir_b + omega_e * psi_r_a;

    voltage_source<>(v_s_a) [sa, gnd];
    L Lls_a<Lls>()          [sa, ma];
    L Lm_a<Lm>()            [ma, qa];
    voltage_source<>(v_sat_a) [qa, gnd];
    L Llr_a<Llr>()          [xa, ma];
    voltage_source<>(v_r_a) [xa, gnd];

    voltage_source<>(v_s_b) [sb, gnd];
    L Lls_b<Lls>()          [sb, mb];
    L Lm_b<Lm>()            [mb, qb];
    voltage_source<>(v_sat_b) [qb, gnd];
    L Llr_b<Llr>()          [xb, mb];
    voltage_source<>(v_r_b) [xb, gnd];

    // ── Stator currents → terminals ─────────────────────────────────────────
    double i_wa = is_a;
    double i_wb = -0.5 * is_a + ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * is_b;
    double i_wc = -0.5 * is_a - ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * is_b;
    double i_a = i_wa;
    double i_b = i_wb;
    double i_c = i_wc;
    if (delta > 0.5) {
        i_a = i_wa - i_wc;
        i_b = i_wb - i_wa;
        i_c = i_wc - i_wb;
    }
    current_source<>(i_a) [va, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_b) [vb, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_c) [vc, gnd];

    // ── Rotor currents → rotor frame → slip rings ───────────────────────────
    double ir_x =  ir_a * c_th + ir_b * s_th;
    double ir_y = -ir_a * s_th + ir_b * c_th;
    double i_ra = ir_x;
    double i_rb = -0.5 * ir_x + ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * ir_y;
    double i_rc = -0.5 * ir_x - ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * ir_y;
    current_source<>(i_ra) [ra, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_rb) [rb, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_rc) [rc, gnd];

    // ── Electromagnetic torque, into the shaft ──────────────────────────────
    // T = 1.5·p·(ψsα·isβ − ψsβ·isα) = 1.5·p·(ψmα·isβ − ψmβ·isα)
    double torque = 1.5 * p * (psi_m_a * is_b - psi_m_b * is_a);
    current_source<>(torque) [gnd, shaft];

    omega_m_out     = omega_m;
    i_alpha_out     = is_a;
    i_beta_out      = is_b;
    psi_r_alpha_out = psi_r_a;
    psi_r_beta_out  = psi_r_b;
    theta_e_out     = theta_r;
    torque_out      = torque;
}
